.\" Written from docs/contracts/C2-4-kbootset-cli.md version 16 and
.\" docs/spec/40-generations-and-composer.md; installed as
.\" /opt/amix/man/1m/kbootset (userland/packages/docs/man-pages.md).
.\" Plain -man requests only: the stock nroff of 1991 formats it.
.\" MIT licence, see LICENSE in kernel/dlm.
.TH KBOOTSET 1M "2026-10-30" "kbootset" "Amix kernel modules"
.nh
.SH NAME
kbootset \- compose, commit and roll back boot generations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kbootset
[\fB\-R\fP \fIroot\fP]
[\fB\-\-image\fP \fIfile\fP | \fB\-\-dev\fP \fIpath\fP]
[\fB\-\-offset\fP \fIn\fP]
[\fB\-\-journal\fP \fIpath\fP]
.I command
[\fIargument\fP ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kbootset
keeps the boot store: the
.B UNI\e0
boot slice that the Amix boot loader reads.
The store holds
.IR generations .
A generation is one kernel with its root-class modules, the manifest that
names them, and the boot- and demand-class modules the manifest names under
.BI /stand/gen/ id /mod.
The loader boots one generation; its menu, safe mode and the try-once rule
choose another when asked or when one fails.
.PP
A generation is in one of four states:
.TP 10
.B pristine
the generation the install laid down; never deleted and never marked bad.
.TP
.B good
it completed a full multi-user boot.
.TP
.B trial
committed and not yet good.
The loader boots a trial once; if that boot does not reach the end of run
level 2, the next boot takes the previous good generation.
.TP
.B bad
a trial that failed, or a generation marked bad by hand.
.PP
The
.I kept set
is the pristine generation, the two newest good generations and at most one
trial.
.B commit
and
.B collect
remove every other generation.
.PP
After a transaction that changes a kernel-space package,
.B apkg
runs
.B "kbootset build"
and
.B "kbootset commit"
itself when
.B bootset=auto
in
.BR /etc/default/bootset ,
and prints the two commands otherwise.
The base system runs
.BR namelist ,
.B retry
and
.B mark-good
on every boot.
.SH OPTIONS
Without
.B \-\-image
or
.BR \-\-dev ,
.B kbootset
works on the running box.
It reads the box's identity from
.B /dev/kinfo
(and makes the node, character 53 0, mode 0444, when it is missing),
and finds the boot store from the
.B store=
line of
.BR /etc/default/bootset ,
else from the loader record, else by scanning every disk.
.TP 10
.BI \-R " root"
Work on a mounted target: the packages, the records and
.B /stand
under
.IR root ,
and the store named by
.IB root /etc/default/bootset
or by
.BR \-\-bootdev .
The installer and repair mode use it.
.TP
.BI \-\-image " file"
The store is in the disk image
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-\-dev " path"
The store is on the raw device
.IR path .
.TP
.BI \-\-offset " n"
The store starts
.I n
bytes into the image or device (default 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-journal " path"
The commit journal.
The default is
.IB root /var/kbootset/journal
on the box; with
.B \-\-image
or
.B \-\-dev
there is none unless one is named.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP 10
.B status
Prints the store header and its set id
.RB ( store-id: ),
the control block, every generation with its state and members, the
booted generation, the loader that booted it and the kernel
(from kinfo), each module of the booted generation as loaded or not
(and why, in a safe boot), whether
.B /stand/unix
is the booted kernel's namelist, and the box's facts.
It writes nothing.
.TP
.B build \fR[\fP\fIoptions\fP\fR]\fP
Composes the next generation from the packages installed under the root
into
.BI /stand/gen/ id
and runs every gate (see GATES).
It writes nothing to the store.
The box's facts come from the options, then kinfo, then
.BR /etc/default/bootset :
.RS
.TP 16
.B "\-\-line 30\fR|\fP46"
the kernel line.
.TP
.BI \-\-bootdev " key"
the boot disk's controller, as a match key
.RB ( zorro:0202:54 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-rootdev " key"
the root disk's controller.
.TP
.BI \-\-rootfs " type"
the root file system type
.RB ( ufs ,
.BR s5 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-mem " bytes"
the box's fast memory.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " record"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-\-swap " record"
.PD
the root and swap records of the manifest, given once on the pristine
build; later builds carry them from the default generation.
.RE
.IP
When the built generation equals the default one,
.B build
says that
.B commit
will write nothing.
.TP
.B commit
Writes the built generation into the store as the trial: the journal, the
members, a read-back of every extent, the manifest, then the control
block, which is the commit point.
It prints
.B "generation N committed; power-cycle to boot it."
When the generation equals the default one (the same line, kabi, root,
swap, members by name, size and SHA-256, and parameters), it writes
nothing and prints
.BR "the default generation M already has these members; nothing to commit" .
On the running box it then makes the disk nodes, as
.B nodes
does.
.TP
.B "commit \-\-again"
Commits the built generation even when it equals the default: a fresh
copy as a new generation, the repair for store bytes that
.B check
found damaged.
.TP
.BI "commit \-\-spec" " spec"
Commits the generation a spec file gives, in the format of the host tool
.BR mkstore ;
for tests and media built without packages.
.TP
.B check
Recomputes the SHA-256 and CRC-32 of every member in the store and of
every file the manifests name under the root, and compares the installed
packages with the default generation.
It never writes.
Exit status 1 when it finds a problem.
.TP
.B mark-good \fR[\fP\fB\-\-booted\fP \fIid\fP\fR] [\fP\fB\-\-secondary\fP\fR]\fP
Run once per boot, as the last script of run level 2.
When the booted generation (from kinfo's loader record) is the trial, it
becomes good and the default; on any other boot nothing is marked.
After a new boot loader's trial boot it accepts the new loader, or reports
that the secondary loader booted.
When the loader passed over the default by itself, it verifies the default:
damaged members mark it bad, and the booted generation becomes the
default.
It also writes the namelist, as
.B namelist
does.
.B \-\-booted
and
.B \-\-secondary
replace the loader record's answers.
After a boot from a CD or a floppy pair it marks nothing.
.TP
.B retry \fR[\fP\fB\-\-booted\fP \fIid\fP\fR]\fP
Run by the base system just before the reboot that follows a root file
system repair.
When the booted generation is the trial, its attempt does not count, at
most twice per trial.
It writes the boot store's control block and nothing on the root;
otherwise it does nothing.
.TP
.B rollback
Makes the newest good generation older than the default the new default,
or the pristine one when there is none.
A pending trial is cancelled.
.TP
.BI mark-bad " id"
Marks generation
.I id
bad.
The pristine generation cannot be marked bad.
.TP
.B next previous\fR|\fPsafe\fR|\fPdevload\fR|\fPnone\fR|\fP\fIid\fP
A request for the next boot only:
the previous good generation, safe mode, development loading (modules
outside the manifest may load, and the loader and kernel say so), no
request, or generation
.IR id .
.TP
.B collect
Removes the generations outside the kept set.
.TP
.BI loader-install " boot2" " \fR[\fP\fB\-\-version\fP \fIn\fP\fR] [\fP\fB\-\-boot1\fP \fIfile\fP\fR]\fP"
Installs a new boot loader as the primary one; the old primary becomes the
secondary, which boot1 runs when the new one does not complete a boot.
boot1 is replaced only with
.BR \-\-boot1 .
.B \-\-version
is the version of the given boot2 (default: this kbootset's own).
.TP
.BI create " size boot2" " \fR[\fP\fB\-\-boot1\fP \fIfile\fP\fR] [\fP\fB\-\-version\fP \fIn\fP\fR] [\fP\fB\-\-force\fP\fR]\fP"
Lays an empty store of
.I size
bytes with the loader
.IR boot2 ;
it needs
.B \-\-dev
or
.BR \-\-image .
A slice that already holds a store or a bootblock is refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given; the first 64 KiB are zeroed first.
.TP
.B namelist
Writes the booted kernel's namelist to
.BR /stand/unix ,
the file
.BR netstat ,
.B ifconfig
and
.B crash
read symbols from: an ELF file of the booted kernel's symbols at their
addresses in this boot.
It writes only when the content differs, as a new file renamed into place.
Exit status 3 when it cannot make one; the line says why.
.TP
.B nodes
Makes the disk nodes a four-card kernel needs
.RB ( /dev/dsk
and
.B /dev/rdsk
c0 to c1f, d0, s0 to s7), removes the d1 nodes, and registers the change to
the
.B core
package on the running box.
It does nothing unless
.B sdcards=4
is in
.B /stand/k30/kabi
or
.BR /stand/k46/kabi .
It needs neither kinfo nor a store.
.TP
.BI major " name"
Prints the majors that the major registry gives module
.IR name ,
one line each,
.BI c " n"
for a character major and
.BI b " n"
for a block major, with nothing else on the line:
.B "kbootset major z3660eth"
prints
.BR "c 51" .
It reads neither the store nor kinfo nor the root, so it answers the same
on a static kernel, in safe mode and after a boot from a CD or a floppy pair.
Exit status 1, with a message on the standard error and nothing on the
standard output, for a name the registry does not know.
.TP
.B "media \fR...\fP"
Builds the pristine generation of a new medium (a disk image, a floppy
pair, a CD tree).
Only the host build of
.B kbootset
has it.
.SH GATES
.B build
and
.B commit
refuse, with exit status 4 and a line naming the gate, when:
.TP 6
1
a kernel-space member's SHA-256 is not the one
.B apkg
recorded at install (hash);
.TP
2
the members disagree on the kernel ABI, or a module needs a symbol that
neither the kernel nor a module it depends on gives (kabi);
.TP
3
no driver in the kernel or the root-class modules covers the boot disk's
controller, the root disk's controller or the root file system, or a
module is missing its board, its majors or a dependency (closure);
.TP
4
the root-class modules do not fit the kernel's arena (arena);
.TP
5
the generation does not fit the free store space beside the kept set
(store space);
.TP
6
the box's fast memory is too small for the kernel, the arena and the
RAM disk (memory);
.TP
7
the installed loader is older than the generation needs (loader);
.TP
8
the boot store cannot be identified unambiguously (boot disk);
.TP
9
the boot disk carries more than one
.B UNI\e0
slice (one boot slice);
.TP
10
the kernel's line does not match the box's processor (line).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
0
done: the store changed as asked, or there was nothing to do.
.TP
1
an I/O error, a read-back mismatch, or a damaged store.
A commit interrupted before its commit point leaves the store unchanged,
and the next run reports the journal.
.TP
2
usage.
.TP
3
refused by a rule: a read-only store, a pristine generation marked bad, no
older good generation, an unknown booted generation.
.TP
4
refused by a gate (see GATES).
.PP
Nothing is written when the status is 2, 3 or 4.
Every line
.B kbootset
prints for a person starts with
.BR "kbootset: " ;
the lines of
.B major
are for scripts and do not.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 16
.BI KBOOTSET_HOLD= n
.B commit
announces each step on the standard error
.RB ( "kbootset: step N" ),
and with this set it syncs and waits before step
.IR n ,
for power-cut tests.
.SH FILES
.TP 28
.B /etc/default/bootset
.B bootset=auto
or
.BR manual ,
the store
.RB ( store= ,
.BR store-id= )
and the box's facts
.RB ( line= ,
.BR bootdev= ,
.BR rootdev= ,
.BR rootfs= ,
.BR mem= ).
.TP
.BI /stand/gen/ id /
a built generation: its manifest and
.BR mod/ ,
the modules the kernel loads from the root.
.TP
.B /stand/unix
the booted kernel's namelist.
.TP
.B /var/kbootset/journal
the commit journal.
.TP
.B /dev/kinfo
the booted kernel's identity, loader record and loaded modules.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/\fIname\fP
an installed module object.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/modreg.d/\fIname\fP
a module's parameters from its package;
.B /etc/conf/modreg.local.d/\fIname\fP
holds the owner's, which win.
.SH EXAMPLES
After installing a kernel-space package by hand with
.BR bootset=manual :
.PP
.RS
.nf
kbootset build
kbootset commit
.fi
.RE
.PP
then power-cycle the box.
To make the previous good generation the default again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kbootset rollback
.fi
.RE
.PP
A boot script that re-creates a missing node:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set \-\- `kbootset major z3660eth`
mknod /dev/zen0 $1 $2 0
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apkg (1)
.SH NOTES
A real 68060 is power-cycled to boot a new generation, not rebooted in
software.
